 }
 
+/// Break BOTH halves of the cursor row's soft-wrap linkage: the row no longer
+/// continues onto the row below it (that is `cursorResetWrap`), and it is no
+/// longer the continuation of the row above it either.
+///
+/// NEX (kelpi #165): this is what EL 2 (`ESC[2K`) needs, and `cursorResetWrap`
+/// on its own is not enough for it. `cursorResetWrap` clears the FORWARD flag
+/// (`row.wrap` here, `row.wrap_continuation` on the next row) and leaves the
+/// BACKWARD one (`row.wrap_continuation` here, `row.wrap` on the previous row)
+/// standing. An `ESC[2K`'d row keeps nothing of what it held, so a reader that
+/// still believes it is the tail of the logical line above is reading a link to
+/// content that no longer exists, and PageList's column reflow is exactly such
+/// a reader:
+///
+/// EL 2 is the ONLY caller. The erases that also start at column 0 but leave
+/// part of the row, or the row's meaning, behind are listed under "what EL 0
+/// deliberately does not do" below, and they keep upstream's behaviour.
+///
+///   - `PageList.ReflowCursor.reflowRow` (`src/terminal/PageList.zig:1028`)
+///     continues the PREVIOUS logical line rather than starting a new one for
+///     any source row carrying `wrap_continuation` (it is the flag it copies
+///     forward at `:1101-1104`), so on a *widen* the erased row's later,
+///     unrelated contents are glued horizontally onto the row above it instead
+///     of standing up as their own row. With a TUI that answers SIGWINCH by
+///     erasing every row and repainting in place (Ink's `eraseLines()` is
+///     `ESC[2K` + `ESC[1A` per row, so every row on screen), the whole frame
+///     comes back as fixed-stride fragments separated by blank runs.
+///   - the same function drops a blank row outright when it is flagged as a
+///     continuation (`src/terminal/PageList.zig:1084`,
+///     `if (!src_row.wrap_continuation) { self.new_rows += 1; }`), so an
+///     `ESC[2K` in the middle of a wrapped run makes the blank row vanish on
+///     the next widen and shifts everything below it up a line.
+///
+/// xterm does clear it, which is the behaviour the daemon's own emulator has
+/// and therefore the parity target for this engine: xterm.js models wrap with
+/// a single backward flag, `BufferLine.isWrapped`, and
+/// `InputHandler._eraseInBufferLine(y, start, end, clearWrap, respectProtect)`
+/// ends with `clearWrap && (line.isWrapped = false)`. Its `eraseInLine` passes
+/// `clearWrap = (x === 0)` for EL 0, `false` for EL 1 and `true` for EL 2
+/// (`@xterm/headless` 6.0.0, `lib-headless/xterm-headless.js`:
+/// `eraseInLine(e,t=!1){...case 0:this._eraseInBufferLine(y,x,cols,0===x,t)...
+/// case 2:this._eraseInBufferLine(y,0,cols,!0,t)}`). Upstream ghostty's
+/// `Terminal.eraseLine` carries the opposite note on its `.complete` arm ("it
+/// seems like complete should reset the soft-wrap state of the line but in
+/// xterm it does not"), which holds for xterm(1)'s own line flags but not for
+/// the xterm.js lineage the daemon runs, and it is what leaves the two VTs
+/// disagreeing after a shrink/repaint/widen.
+///
+/// ## WHAT EL 0 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO, AND WHAT THAT IS AND IS NOT WORTH
+///
+/// Only `Terminal.eraseLine`'s `.complete` arm (EL 2) calls this. EL 0 with the
+/// cursor at column 0 erases the same cells, and xterm.js clears its flag
+/// there too, and we still do NOT. Be clear about what that buys, because it is
+/// less than it first looks.
+///
+/// `CR` + erase + rewrite is how a progress bar, a spinner or a status line
+/// redraws the tail of a line it has already printed, and when that line has
+/// soft-wrapped the row it lands on is a continuation row. Breaking the
+/// backward linkage there tears the logical line on the next widen. Keeping EL
+/// 0 forward-only means this engine still rejoins it. Measured on this wasm,
+/// `0123456789ABCDEF` at 10 columns then `CR ESC[K ABCDEF` then a widen to 30
+/// gives `0123456789ABCDEF` here and on upstream, and gave `0123456789` +
+/// `ABCDEF` while the EL 0 arm was in.
+///
+/// TWO THINGS THAT DOES NOT MEAN.
+///
+/// 1. It does not mean "the progress-bar case is handled". The same idiom
+///    spelled with EL 2 still tears, and EL 2 ignores the cursor column so
+///    either ordering does it: `CR ESC[2K` and `ESC[2K CR` both come back as
+///    `0123456789` + `ABCDEF` after the widen, where upstream rejoined. That
+///    spelling is not exotic, it is `ansi-escapes`' `eraseLine` and so most
+///    Node spinners including `ora`. It is also CORRECT on the grounds this
+///    whole patch rests on: `@xterm/headless` clears `isWrapped` for EL 2, and
+///    `addon-serialize` emits the two halves as two rows, so after this change
+///    the client and the authoritative screen finally AGREE about that line.
+///    Tearing in both is better than tearing in one.
+/// 2. It does not mean the rejoin survives. It is a live-engine-only property.
+///    The daemon's emulator clears `isWrapped` for EL 0 at column 0 as well
+///    (`clearWrap = (x === 0)`), and its snapshot serialises the halves as two
+///    rows (measured: `"0123456789\r\nABCDEF"`), so the next settled-resize
+///    replay re-tears the line on the client anyway.
+///
+/// So the honest statement of this non-change is: a knowing divergence from the
+/// authoritative screen that happens to match upstream ghostty, costs nothing,
+/// and pre-dates this patch. It is kept because #165 does not need it (Ink's
+/// `eraseLines()` and ratatui/crossterm's `Clear(CurrentLine)` are both
+/// `ESC[2K`, so the `.complete` arm alone closes the reported bug) and because
+/// changing it would make the live engine worse between replays for no gain.
+/// Pinned in `packages/client/src/terminal/vendor-engine.test.ts` and by
+/// "Terminal: eraseLine right leaves a continuation row's backward linkage
+/// alone" below.
+///
+/// EL 1 (`.left`, `ESC[1K`) is likewise untouched, and so therefore is
+/// `ESC[1J` (ED `.above`), which delegates its cursor row to
+/// `eraseLine(.left)`. xterm.js DOES clear the flag for its EL 1 and ED 1, so
+/// that is the same kind of divergence, with the same replay caveat: an erase
+/// that leaves the right-hand side of the row standing has not emptied the row,
+/// and the rows below it are still its continuation.
+///
+/// ## DECSEL
+///
+/// `ESC[?2K` on a row of protected cells erases nothing, and this still breaks
+/// the linkage, so "a row erased from column 0 keeps nothing of what it held"
+/// is not true on that path. It is xterm.js parity and intentional:
+/// `_eraseInBufferLine` applies `clearWrap` OUTSIDE its `respectProtect`
+/// branch, so xterm.js clears `isWrapped` for `ESC[?2K` as well.
+pub fn cursorResetWrapFull(self: *Screen) void {
+    // The forward half, unchanged: pending wrap, this row's `wrap`, the next
+    // row's `wrap_continuation`, and this row's trailing spacer head.
+    self.cursorResetWrap();
+
+    // The backward half. Nothing to do if this row was never a continuation;
+    // in particular this leaves a row that legitimately starts a logical line
+    // completely untouched, so the only rows this can affect are ones reflow
+    // itself linked up.
+    const page_row = self.cursor.page_row;
+    if (!page_row.wrap_continuation) return;
+    page_row.wrap_continuation = false;
+
+    // The row above may live in a different page, so go through the pin the
+    // same way `splitCellBoundary` does when it has to reach the previous row
+    // (see the `wrap_continuation` / `spacer_head` block in that function).
+    const prev = self.cursor.page_pin.up(1) orelse return;
+    const prev_rac = prev.rowAndCell();
+    prev_rac.row.wrap = false;
+
+    // A wide character split across the row boundary leaves a spacer head in
+    // the last cell of the row above. With the linkage gone there is no tail
+    // for it to point at, so clear it exactly as `cursorResetWrap` clears the
+    // spacer head on its own row.
+    const prev_cells = prev.cells(.all);
+    const prev_cols = prev.node.data.size.cols;
+    if (prev_cells[prev_cols - 1].wide == .spacer_head) {
+        self.clearCells(
+            &prev.node.data,
+            prev_rac.row,
+            prev_cells[prev_cols - 1 ..][0..1],
+        );
+    }
+
+    // We may have changed a cell on the row above, so it has to be repainted.
+    prev_rac.row.dirty = true;
+}
